    module: 1,
    domain: "Craft and Structure",
    passage: "Passage A\nThe rise of artificial intelligence in the workplace has generated understandable anxiety about job displacement. Historical parallels suggest that while some jobs will be eliminated, new categories of work will emerge, just as the Industrial Revolution created jobs that could not have been imagined in the pre-industrial era. The key challenge, then, is not preventing technological change but managing the transition through education and training programs that prepare workers for the jobs of the future.\n\nPassage B\nThe historical analogy between the Industrial Revolution and the AI revolution is misleading. The Industrial Revolution primarily replaced physical labor, and displaced workers could often retrain for new types of physical work. AI, by contrast, targets cognitive labor—the very skills that education systems have traditionally developed. Moreover, the pace of change is far faster today, leaving less time for workers and institutions to adapt. The transition challenge is not simply a matter of more training but of fundamentally rethinking what kinds of skills will be valuable in an AI-driven economy.",
