    domain: "Craft and Structure",
    passage: "Passage A\nThe subjective experience of beauty is not merely a matter of personal preference but is rooted in evolutionary biology. Research has shown that humans across cultures tend to find certain patterns and proportions aesthetically pleasing. Symmetry, for example, is almost universally preferred, likely because it signals health and genetic fitness. Landscapes with open vistas, water, and greenery are consistently rated as beautiful, perhaps because such environments were favorable to human survival. Our aesthetic preferences, in this view, are shaped by the evolutionary pressures that shaped our species.\n\nPassage B\nEvolutionary explanations of beauty are appealing but suffer from a fundamental problem: they are difficult to test. When we observe that people prefer symmetrical faces, we can invent an evolutionary story about why this preference developed, but we cannot easily falsify such an account. Moreover, evolutionary explanations struggle to account for the vast cultural variation in aesthetic preferences. What is considered beautiful in one culture may be unremarkable or even ugly in another. The human preference for symmetry, for example, is not universal—some cultures actively value asymmetry in art and design. A truly adequate account of beauty must incorporate both biological and cultural factors.",
